            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"The Church, often traditionally conceptualized as the 'Barque of Peter' or typologically linked to Noah's Ark, which carries the faithful safely through the turbulent and dangerous waters of the world toward salvation.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"1 Peter 3:20-21\",\n    \"Luke 5:3-11\",\n    \"Mark 4:35-41\",\n    \"Matthew 8:23-27\",\n    \"Genesis 6:14-22\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Worldly commerce, human pride, and the fleeting wealth of economic Babylon (often specifically the 'ships of Tarshish').\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Revelation 18:17-19\",\n        \"Isaiah 2:16\",\n        \"Ezekiel 27:25-36\",\n        \"Psalm 48:7\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Christ Himself as the ultimate refuge and vessel of salvation, into whom believers must enter to be saved from the deluge of God's judgment.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Hebrews 11:7\",\n        \"John 6:21\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"The individual human life or soul navigating the temporal journey and storms of mortal existence, guided by God to a safe harbor.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Psalm 107:23-30\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"minority\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": true,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"The symbolism of the ship as the Church is deeply embedded in Christian tradition, evidenced by the architectural term 'nave' (from Latin 'navis', meaning ship) used for the main body of a church building where the congregation gathers. While the ship as the Church is a positive symbol of salvation, the prophets and Revelation frequently use merchant ships (especially ships of Tarshish) as negative symbols of idolatrous reliance on wealth, human industry, and pride.\"\n}",
